"""Installment 10, wave 09: Gabriel Okara's 1946-2000 bibliography (African writing in English).

The second African author of installment 10, and the first of the African, Caribbean and
South Asian founders recorded here whose works include poetry. Okara's books of the period are recorded: the novel
The Voice (1964), the collection The Fisherman's Invocation (1978) and the two books for
children of 1992, together with his first published poem, "The Call of the River Nun"
(written 1950, prized 1953, printed 1957). They were checked against the Wikipedia articles
on Okara, The Voice and "You Laughed and Laughed and Laughed"; James M. Manheim's article in
Gale's Contemporary Black Biography and Howard Sergeant's in Contemporary Poets (both read
on Encyclopedia.com); the introduction to Chukwuma Azuonye's 2006 interview with Okara; the
catalogue contents of the 2016 Collected Poems; and catalogue records for the children's
books. None of the books was read.

Okara was planned after Cyprian Ekwensi. He comes first because his output in the period is
small enough to be recorded and checked in one wave, while Ekwensi's forty-odd novels,
novellas and children's books need a longer bibliographic pass.

Sources disagree on his birth date (21 or 24 April 1921), on when he studied journalism at
Northwestern University (1949, 1956 or 1959-1960), on his posts in Rivers State after the
civil war, on whether the 1979 Commonwealth Poetry Prize went to the book or to its title
poem, and on the date of Little Snake and Little Frog (1981 or 1992); each record states the
disagreement. The plot of The Voice beyond what the sources give is from general knowledge
of the novel and is labelled so. Every work is linked to "African Literature in English",
whose taxonomy entry names Okara; no other movement is assigned.
"""

